*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !netdefs(mode, defs, err)
*
*    READ   defs is returned as a dynamic array, ONE DEFINITION PER ATTRIBUTE, the eight fields of a definition separated by
*           value marks.  Nothing is validated beyond splitting the line at the vertical bars: a line that does not hold eight
*           fields comes back as it is (the caller counts them), blank lines are dropped, and a mark byte found in a line is
*           replaced by a question mark so that it cannot make a new attribute.  No file at all is an empty defs and no error.
*           Anyone may call it; it takes no lock (the file is replaced whole, so a reader sees the old file or the new one).
*    WRITE  administrator only (err 2001 otherwise).  Writes defs, in the same shape, as the whole new file: a copy under
*           netservers.new, mode 644 (the umask is set to 022 for the creation and put back), then renamed over the old file, so
*           the file is never half written.  A definition that is not eight fields, or a field with a vertical bar, a control
*           character or a byte above 126, is refused (err 12441 with the number of the attribute) and nothing is written.
*           THE CALLER TAKES THE LOCK across its read, change and write: readu on the id netservers.lock in @sdsys (no
*           such record is ever made; the lock is the point), as set.server and delete.server do.
*
*    err is null or  message number : value mark : its arguments :  12432 cannot read the file, 12433 cannot write it,
*    12441 a definition that cannot be written, 2001 not the administrator, 2018 an unknown mode.
*
* THE FILE:  netservers in the SDSYS directory (@sdsys), plain text, one definition per line, lines ending in a newline:
*
*    name|host|port|account|pin|mode|accounts|created
*
*    name      lower case letter then up to 15 lower case letters or digits
*    host      DNS name, IPv4 address or IPv6 literal
*    port      1 to 65535, the TARGET port, chosen by whoever defines the server
*    account   the account to enter on the remote server
*    pin       the SHA-256 hash of the server's public key (SPKI), 64 lower case hex digits
*    mode      read or write
*    accounts  the LOCAL accounts that may use the server, comma separated, null for none
*    created   yyyy-mm-dd
*
* THE FILE MUST NEVER HOLD A SECRET.  It is readable by everybody (mode 644) and only the sdsys user writes it.  A name, an
* address, a port and the hash of a PUBLIC key are not secrets; a password, a key or a token is not to be added to it, ever.
* Credentials, when a later slice has them, live elsewhere, outside every account's directory, in a file only their owner can read.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine netdefs(mode, defs, err)
$catalog !netdefs

$include int$keys.h

$define NETDEFS.FILE   'netservers'      ;* in the gcat directory of @sdsys (see below)
$define NETDEFS.FIELDS 8

   err = ''
   mode = upcase(mode)
*  25 Oct 26 Windows port - WHERE THE FILE LIVES.  On Linux the file is mode 644 in the SDSYS directory, which only the sdsys user can write.
*  On Windows the SDSYS directory itself gives the group sdusers Modify (inherited), so a file made in it could be rewritten by any SD user
*  with an operating system prompt.  The gcat directory under it is Administrators-only for writing (the installer's own ACL: sdusers read
*  and execute only), so the definitions are kept there; the SDSYS session that defines a server is elevated and can write it.
   path = @sdsys:@ds:'gcat':@ds:NETDEFS.FILE

   begin case
      case mode = 'READ'
         defs = ''
         if ospath(path, OS$EXISTS) then
            openseq path to f else
               err = 12432:@vm:path:@vm:status() ;* Cannot read %1 (status %2)
               return
            end
            n = 0
            loop
               readseq line from f else exit
               if trim(line) # '' then
*                 A mark byte in a line would make a second attribute or field out of one: it cannot be one of ours.
                  convert char(251):char(252):char(253):char(254):char(255) to '?????' in line
                  convert '|' to @vm in line
                  n += 1
                  defs<n> = line
               end
            repeat
            closeseq f
         end

      case mode = 'WRITE'
         if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
            err = 2001 ;* Command requires administrator privileges
            return
         end

*        Look at every definition before the first byte is written: a refusal leaves the file as it was.
         n = dcount(defs, @fm)
         for i = 1 to n
            one = defs<i>
            ok = (dcount(one, @vm) = NETDEFS.FIELDS)
            if ok then
               for j = 1 to len(one)
                  c = seq(one[j, 1])
                  if c # 253 and (c < 32 or c > 126 or c = 124) then ok = @false
               next j
            end
            if not(ok) then
               err = 12441:@vm:i ;* Server definitions file: line %1 is not a definition
               return
            end
         next i

         tmp = path : '.new'
         if ospath(tmp, OS$EXISTS) then
            if not(ospath(tmp, OS$DELETE)) then
               err = 12433:@vm:tmp:@vm:status() ;* Cannot write %1 (status %2)
               return
            end
         end

*        Mode 644: the umask is 022 while the file is made, and is put back whatever happens.
         old.umask = umask(-1)
         void umask(18)
         openseq tmp to tmp.f then
            weofseq tmp.f
            made = @true
         end else
            made = @true
            create tmp.f else made = @false
         end
         void umask(old.umask)
         if not(made) then
            err = 12433:@vm:tmp:@vm:status()
            return
         end

         for i = 1 to n
            line = defs<i>
            convert @vm to '|' in line
            writeseq line to tmp.f else
               err = 12433:@vm:tmp:@vm:status()
               exit
            end
         next i
         closeseq tmp.f

         if err # '' then
            void ospath(tmp, OS$DELETE)
            return
         end
         if not(osrename(tmp, path)) then
            err = 12433:@vm:path:@vm:status()
            void ospath(tmp, OS$DELETE)
         end

      case 1
         err = 2018:@vm:mode   ;* Unexpected token (xx)
   end case

   return

end

* END-CODE
